/*
Interpretation:
- There are N vertical lines.
- Each lottery acts as a permutation of the N lines.
- A horizontal segment on line i:
    0 = connected to left  => swap(i-1, i)
    1 = connected to right => swap(i, i+1)
  (with 0-based indexing, valid only when neighbor exists)
- Applying all horizontal lines from top to bottom gives one permutation.
- We may choose one or more given lotteries and stack them in any order.
- Need to know whether some concatenation becomes a "good" Amidakuji,
  i.e. identity permutation.

Since we may use each lottery at most once and reorder arbitrarily, the
resulting permutation is the product of some subset in some order.
A necessary and sufficient invariant here is parity:
every adjacent swap flips permutation parity, so each lottery has fixed parity.
Identity is even, hence total chosen odd-parity count must be even.

If there exists at least one even lottery, choosing just that one works only if
its whole permutation is identity, so parity alone is not enough.

However, because the statement only provides each horizontal line as left/right
moves, a single lottery path for all lines defines a permutation, and we need
whether some non-empty product can be identity.

We can model all permutations generated by the given lotteries and check whether
identity is reachable by multiplying distinct chosen lotteries in any order.
That is subset DP over permutations. Since constraints are omitted, we assume
N is small enough for permutation-state DP.

State: map permutation -> reachable after using some subset mask.
Transition: append an unused lottery.
Stop if identity reached with non-empty subset.

This is exponential in M, so we instead do DP over subsets storing resulting
permutations obtainable by ordering that subset. For each subset, generate from
subset without one element. This is still expensive but workable only for small M.

As constraints are not given, a safer practical assumption in such problems is
small M and N. We'll implement exact search with memoization:
dfs(mask, perm) is implicit by BFS over states (mask, perm-rank).

Permutation ranking compresses states.

If identity is reachable with mask != 0 => yes, else no.

Input format assumed:
N M
For each lottery:
  K
  then K integers (0/1), one per horizontal line

If a move would go out of bounds, that lottery description is invalid under this
interpretation; we simply ignore impossible swaps.

*/

static long long rank_perm(const vector<int>& p) {
    int n = (int)p.size();
    long long rank = 0;
    vector<int> used(n, 0);
    vector<long long> fact(n + 1, 1);
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) fact[i] = fact[i - 1] * i;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        int cnt = 0;
        for (int x = 0; x < p[i]; ++x) if (!used[x]) cnt++;
        rank += cnt * fact[n - 1 - i];
        used[p[i]] = 1;
    }
    return rank;
}

